<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Deutsche Welle: DW.com</title><subtitle type="html">Deutsche Welle: DW.com</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-asia</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-asia" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12758?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-asia" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Fifty years after Mao’s death, China has transformed. DW looks at why his political legacy still matters.</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:73bcb6e9545d5167a6a45c347fb0556726ed33c3ed08ec1802437087429c6b38</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-08T14:44:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/fifty-years-after-mao-s-death-china-has-transformed-dw-looks-at-why-his-political-legacy-still-matte/a-79189751--6efb4c761178.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/fifty-years-after-mao-s-death-china-has-transformed-dw-looks-at-why-his-political-legacy-still-matters/a-79189751?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Mao Zedong was perhaps the most influential, and certainly one of the most controversial, figures in 20th-century China. Even five decades after his death, the legacy of his era continues to influence China today.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Japan's empty temples seek new ways to survive</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9a3d078390c946e32e8bb972111540b88f1f3bd46e322a9be0a7ec2e71ee751e</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-08T12:31:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/japan-s-empty-temples-seek-new-ways-to-survive/a-79157964--7eea677a26bf.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/japan-s-empty-temples-seek-new-ways-to-survive/a-79157964?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">With a declining population, shrinking congregations and growing economic pressures, temples are struggling to stay relevant. As more temples are sold or closed, is religion in decline in modern Japan?</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Afghanistan's women: A national soccer team without a home</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f719fc0e9b0b3d9ebe18e91a023f58564bc7940c6038ae1d64c8cc4316ce9561</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-08T11:03:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/afghanistan-s-women-a-national-soccer-team-without-a-home/a-79139500--7cfb666b9c3a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/afghanistan-s-women-a-national-soccer-team-without-a-home/a-79139500?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The Afghan women's national football team has finally been officially recognized by FIFA. Now the players want to make history both on and off the field.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>India news: Hindu group apologizes over Eiffel Tower row</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c3c388de8249e3d984c30ffe6ecba34ecedf05c976da458f31112c5630e346f2</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-08T06:38:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/india-news-hindu-group-apologizes-over-eiffel-tower-row/live-79153937--9288b3e28d91.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/india-news-hindu-group-apologizes-over-eiffel-tower-row/live-79153937?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">After the Eiffel Tower was closed amid a staff strike over alleged discrimination, Hindu group BAPS issued an apology, but maintained that no one was denied access. DW has more.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Eiffel Tower employees strike after female staff removed</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cf8180659cb3879e53028a454de344b6e5e32286abfefea6cafee7e08173560e</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-07T22:23:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/eiffel-tower-employees-strike-after-female-staff-removed/a-79152603--a226c210998d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/eiffel-tower-employees-strike-after-female-staff-removed/a-79152603?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The employees protested against the removal of the site's female workers due to a request by a visiting Hindu group. The tower's operating company apologized for the decision.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Pakistan: Can creating new provinces fix governance?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cb053b5d042b13fdad585b44f6b8c828365bab4e751b1ffe49b87a428ccd9e7e</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-07T13:55:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/pakistan-can-creating-new-provinces-fix-governance/a-79148059--3974ef70363a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/pakistan-can-creating-new-provinces-fix-governance/a-79148059?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Pakistanis broadly agree that the country needs more responsive and effective government, but many question whether simply creating more provinces is the solution.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Russia opens first road bridge with North Korea</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:345e7d218e84a0f0168ab7e0b24b070cb7e92a08534842a4d4bce5efe9664878</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-07T10:16:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/russia-opens-first-road-bridge-with-north-korea/a-79142595--dfd907ad5594.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/russia-opens-first-road-bridge-with-north-korea/a-79142595?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Russia and North Korea have opened their first road bridge across the Tumen River as political, economic and military ties between the two countries deepen.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>India news: Rescue efforts underway in deadly Delhi building collapse</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5de65723065165192d2e84e0301b78682ef978cd202c4c216d6c8b1f5ff9ba53</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-07T07:33:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/india-news-rescue-efforts-underway-in-deadly-delhi-building-collapse/live-79123607--5263e9e58a28.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/india-news-rescue-efforts-underway-in-deadly-delhi-building-collapse/live-79123607?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The building was located in New Delhi's Satya Niketan, popular among students from nearby colleges and universities. At least 12 people have been rescued from the rubble.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>How climate change and food insecurity could fuel future conflicts</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8f04eab3c6bb950309b7f4a82526b088a5f2e1c33e4d5bc70bbb717a6922f373</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-06T07:47:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/how-climate-change-and-food-insecurity-could-fuel-future-conflicts/a-78871431--8e63f64c4115.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/how-climate-change-and-food-insecurity-could-fuel-future-conflicts/a-78871431?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Climate change, conflict, and trade tensions are putting pressure on the global food system. New research suggests the biggest risk is not food shortages, but whether societies can peacefully adapt to change.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Nepal flood disaster puts climate justice under spotlight</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c320e5bd198413ec1282e6e84288abc20a9195945dd8f724b7ddc04e81b8199a</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T13:59:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/nepal-flood-disaster-puts-climate-justice-under-spotlight/a-78998617--8f177ec379f1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/nepal-flood-disaster-puts-climate-justice-under-spotlight/a-78998617?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The devastating Himalayan glacial floods have raised broader questions about who should bear the costs and responsibility for the impacts of climate-related disasters.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Why North Korea's women's football team is so good</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:dc0f5c1c58a28e413128286b71ab37c204e56e610e8b1990b6462c80efae3fba</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T10:01:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/why-north-korea-s-women-s-football-team-is-so-good/a-70313505--6b31ccb5ebf0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/why-north-korea-s-women-s-football-team-is-so-good/a-70313505?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">North Korea's young footballers enter the U-20 World Cup in Poland as defending champions — the 2024 title was their third. Why is the isolated state so good at the game?</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>US sanctions halt TOEFL and GRE tests in Iran, disrupting study plans</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f682212c5d572f56c794488afb00b0084cf541c9719ad206715eb2230bc13c0f</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-03T14:24:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/us-sanctions-halt-toefl-and-gre-tests-in-iran-disrupting-study-plans/a-78764639--4e7bec2b9a4e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/us-sanctions-halt-toefl-and-gre-tests-in-iran-disrupting-study-plans/a-78764639?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Students in Iran can no longer take English-language proficiency exams after the suspension of a US sanctions exemption. Applicants say the move threatens university deadlines and adds costly travel obligations.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Can communities be protected from melting glaciers?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e5b4eb5d11de0c9ea69658784220eee1dcbd4526cfe9c2f4321b1723baee411f</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:30Z</updated><published>2026-09-01T13:18:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/as-glaciers-melt-can-communities-be-protected/a-78615775--a898d3259825.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/can-communities-be-protected-from-melting-glaciers/a-78615775?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Rising temperatures are increasing the risk of floods and avalanches in mountain regions. The disaster in Nepal and Tibet shows the urgent need for solutions, and the tools that could help save lives.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry></feed>
